          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Apollonius’ Circle",
              "marks": [
                "bold"
              ],
              "colour": "#8b5cc7"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " Let ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "A(x_1,y_1)",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " and ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "B(x_2,y_2)",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " be two fixed points in the Cartesian plane. A point ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "P(x,y)",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " satisfies ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "PA=k\\times PB",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", where ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "k>1",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " is a constant.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p109-q79-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that coordinates of $P$ satisfies the equation of a circle.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The locus is a circle if $A\\ne B$. If $A=B$, it is the single point $A$, a degenerate circle.\n\nUse the distance formula, square both sides and complete the square in both variables.",
          "worked": "By the distance formula,\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\sqrt{(x-x_1)^2+(y-y_1)^2}&=k\\sqrt{(x-x_2)^2+(y-y_2)^2},\\\\\n(x-x_1)^2+(y-y_1)^2&=k^2\\big[(x-x_2)^2+(y-y_2)^2\\big].\n\\end{align*}$$\nPut\n$$\\begin{align*}\nD&=k^2-1>0,\\\\\nH&=\\frac{k^2x_2-x_1}{D},\\\\\nK&=\\frac{k^2y_2-y_1}{D}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nExpanding, dividing by $D$ and completing both squares gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\nx^2-2Hx+y^2-2Ky&=\\frac{x_1^2+y_1^2-k^2(x_2^2+y_2^2)}{D},\\\\\n(x-H)^2+(y-K)^2&=H^2+K^2+\\frac{x_1^2+y_1^2-k^2(x_2^2+y_2^2)}{D}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe numerator simplifies using\n$$\\begin{align*}\n(k^2x_2-x_1)^2+D(x_1^2-k^2x_2^2)&=k^2(x_1-x_2)^2,\\\\\n(k^2y_2-y_1)^2+D(y_1^2-k^2y_2^2)&=k^2(y_1-y_2)^2.\n\\end{align*}$$\nHence the completed-square equation is\n$$\\begin{align*}\n(x-H)^2+(y-K)^2&=\\frac{k^2\\big[(x_1-x_2)^2+(y_1-y_2)^2\\big]}{D^2}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nIf $A\\ne B$, the right-hand side is positive, so this is a circle with centre $(H,K)$. Squaring introduces no extra points because both distances are non-negative and $k>0$.\n\nIf $A=B$, then $(H,K)=(x_1,y_1)$ and the radius is $0$. The locus is the single point $P=A=B$, a degenerate circle.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 104
      },
      {
        "id": "p109-q79-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the coordinates of the centre and the radius of this circle in terms of $x_1$, $x_2$, $y_1$, $y_2$ and $k$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Centre:\n$$\\left(\\frac{k^2x_2-x_1}{k^2-1},\\frac{k^2y_2-y_1}{k^2-1}\\right).$$\nRadius:\n$$r=\\frac{k\\sqrt{(x_1-x_2)^2+(y_1-y_2)^2}}{k^2-1}.$$\n\nRead the centre and radius from the completed-square form. The radius is positive if $A\\ne B$ and zero if $A=B$.",
          "worked": "Reading the centre from the completed-square equation in (a) gives\n$$\\left(\\frac{k^2x_2-x_1}{k^2-1},\\frac{k^2y_2-y_1}{k^2-1}\\right).$$\nSince $k>1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\nr^2&=\\frac{k^2\\big[(x_1-x_2)^2+(y_1-y_2)^2\\big]}{(k^2-1)^2},\\\\\nr&=\\frac{k\\sqrt{(x_1-x_2)^2+(y_1-y_2)^2}}{k^2-1}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nFor $A\\ne B$, $r>0$. For $A=B$, the centre is $A$ and $r=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 54
      }
    ]
  },
